============================================================
71. if-else vs switch — INTERVIEW
============================================================

Question:

When should you use switch instead of if-else?

Answer:

Use switch when one expression is being compared against
multiple discrete constant values.

Use if-else when conditions involve:

    ranges
    relational operators
    complex logical expressions


Example:

    marks >= 90

requires if-else.

Example:

    menu choice == 1, 2, 3, 4

is suitable for switch.


============================================================
72. switch LIMITATIONS
============================================================

switch is not ideal for:

    case x > 10

    case x >= 100

    case x == y

    case "hello"

Instead use if-else or another appropriate mechanism.


============================================================
73. break VS continue
============================================================

break:

    Terminates the nearest loop or switch.

continue:

    Skips the rest of the current loop iteration and
    proceeds to the next iteration.

Example:

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        if (i == 2)
            continue;

        printf("%d ", i);
    }


Output:

    0 1 3 4


break is also valid inside switch.

continue is associated with loops.


============================================================
74. return INSIDE if
============================================================

Example:

    int check(int x)
    {
        if (x < 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        return 1;
    }


An early return can simplify decision logic.


============================================================
75. GUARD CLAUSE EXAMPLE
============================================================

Instead of:

    if (ptr != NULL)
    {
        if (size > 0)
        {
            process(ptr, size);
        }
    }


You can write:

    if (ptr == NULL)
        return;

    if (size == 0)
        return;

    process(ptr, size);


This is often easier to maintain.


============================================================
76. CONDITIONAL OPERATOR AND TYPES
============================================================

Remember Chapter 6.

Example:

    int a = 10;
    double b = 20.5;

    double result = condition ? a : b;


The operands may undergo type conversions.

The conditional operator has its own type rules.


============================================================
77. SHORT-CIRCUIT IS NOT THE SAME AS BITWISE
============================================================

Logical:

    a && b

may skip b.


Bitwise:

    a & b

evaluates both operands because it is an arithmetic/bitwise
operation, not logical short-circuiting.


Similarly:

    a || b

can skip b.


    a | b

does not short-circuit.


============================================================
78. SIDE EFFECTS AND CONDITIONS
============================================================

Avoid overly complicated expressions with side effects.

Example:

    if (i++ < 10 && function())
    {
        ...
    }


Although this may be valid, complex side effects can make
control flow harder to understand.

Prefer readable code.


