94. GATE-LEVEL CONCEPT
============================================================

Consider:

    int x = 0;

    if (x = 5)
        printf("A");
    else
        printf("B");


Step 1:

    x = 5


Step 2:

Expression value:

    5


Step 3:

5 is non-zero.

Therefore:

    condition = true


Output:

    A


============================================================
95. GATE-LEVEL CONCEPT
============================================================

Consider:

    int a = 0;
    int b = 10;

    if (a && b++)
    {
        ...
    }

After evaluation:

    a = 0

Therefore:

    a && b++

short-circuits.

So:

    b++

does NOT execute.

Final:

    b = 10


This demonstrates why short-circuit behavior matters.


============================================================
96. GATE-LEVEL CONCEPT
============================================================

Consider:

    int a = 1;
    int b = 10;

    if (a || b++)
    {
        ...
    }

Since:

    a = 1

is true:

    b++

is not evaluated.

Final:

    b = 10


